Examine the following Android `QuizActivity` code snippet:

```java
public class QuizActivity extends AppCompatActivity {
    private int currentScore = 0;

    @Override
    protected void onCreate(Bundle savedInstanceState) {
        super.onCreate(savedInstanceState);
        setContentView(R.layout.activity_quiz);
        // Note: savedInstanceState is not read here
    }

    public void onCorrectAnswerClicked(View view) {
        currentScore += 10;
    }
}
```

A student answers 3 questions (`currentScore` becomes `30`) and then rotates the phone from portrait to landscape mode (with default manifest settings). What is the value of `currentScore` after rotation, and why?

Consider the following two Android `Intent` creations:

```java
// Snippet 1
Intent intentA = new Intent(MainActivity.this, ResultActivity.class);
intentA.putExtra("SCORE", 88);
startActivity(intentA);

// Snippet 2
Intent intentB = new Intent(Intent.ACTION_VIEW, Uri.parse("[https://www.haramaya.edu.et](https://www.haramaya.edu.et)"));
startActivity(intentB);
```

How are `intentA` and `intentB` classified in Android?

